A||||8||Jeff-87||Lost Door Key / trunk lock removal||||9||
Z||000000||Jeff-87||03-15-2001||09:51 AM||||Hi, I bought my first Fiero back in November.  It did not run when I bought it and while loading it up, the door key was lost.  It is a GT and has the rear truck automatic release, so I was able to get to the engine.  I have gotten it running, want to start driving it as soon as the snow goes, but I do not have the door key and do not want to get locked out.  Does anyone know if the lock codes are printed anywhere on the car?  If not, how do I get the rear lock out of the trunk?  I talked to a lock smith and he said that he would make a key.  It is a lot cheaper if I remove a lock for him.  I unscrewed the 4 screws holding the rear lock in, but the lock did not appear to loosen up.   Any help on this is greatly appreciated.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Jeff||||reg||9
Z||000001||862M4inCA||03-15-2001||10:39 AM||||This is a long shot, but I've been told that GM keeps a "record" of key codes based on the VIN. Might be worth a shot to call your local Pontiac dealer and see if they can provide you with the code. Shouldn't be more than $10-15 to have a key cut then.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||03-15-2001||10:50 AM||||That is correct, GM can cut a key by VIN number, HOWEVER, this will NOT WORK if the previous owner changed lock cylinders all together.  If it has original locks then it will work.  It's worth spending the couple of bucks to find out.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Jeff-87||03-15-2001||03:00 PM||||Hi, thanks for the info.  I called GM roadside assistance (800-762-3743) and they have Pontiac lock info all the way back to 1973.  They then send the info to a Pontiac dealer or registered locksmith.<BR>Jeff||||reg||10
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||04-17-2001||06:30 PM||||Jeff:<P>I have the same problem on an 86SE (lost door keys).  I called my local Pontiac dealer and he said his key codes do not go back to 1986.  Were you successful in getting a key made from the VIN.  I sure do not want to change the lock cylinders if I don't have to.<P>TIA<BR>Paul||||reg||1
Z||000005||RNGCRSH||04-17-2001||06:44 PM||||Remove the passenger side lock cylinder (not easy) and take it to a lock smith to decode it will cost you about $25. If you want them to remove the lock cylinder add another $65~$100 (locksmiths hate Fieros, at least around here)||||reg||1
Z||000006||rogergarrison||04-18-2001||11:13 AM||||I get lots of repo cars with no keys. I just have an auto locksmith come out and make a set. I pay $50 for service call and ignition and door key.||||reg||1
Z||000007||PAUL88GT||04-18-2001||10:49 PM||||A local locksmith told me to remove the door lock cylinder and bring to him and he charges $16 to make a new door key.  However, I just don't want to have to remove and replace the door cylinder. It is a very tight fit to replace the clip. Not fun.  I guess you can't get a door key replaced from just the VIN, at least that is what I am finding out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Two88GTs||04-19-2001||12:49 AM||||On a related subject, can a locksmith "recode" a lock cylinder to accept a different key?  I'm asking because one of my 88GTs came with a non-original passenger door and of course the lock cylinder was not changed(no key for the passenger side door came with the car).  <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
